    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Genome-wide association studies have uncovered thousands of single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) that are statistically associated with diseases or phenotypes of interest.  Most efforts to understand these results have focused on SNPs that disrupt coding regions of the genome, despite the fact that a majority of variants are found in noncoding areas. To address this imbalance, we previously developed the RegulomeDB database, which allows for the improved identification and characterization of regulatory SNPs.   RegulomeDB annotates SNPs provided by the user, indicating how likely each is to have a functional effect on gene regulation based on experimental evidence from the Encyclopedia of DNA Elements (ENCODE) Consortium, Gene Expression Omnibus (GEO), and other sources.  However, the scoring system is entirely heuristic, making it difficult to interpret and use the results.  To improve this system, we develop a machine learning based approach to determine whether a SNP without direct experimental evidence is likely to affect transcription factor binding.  This work is the first in a series of steps to improve RegulomeDB as a tool for annotating potentially damaging regulatory SNPs in personal genomes.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
